  "## A poem of the life of Christ",
  "Juvencus was a Spanish priest who, about the year 330, told the story of the gospels in Latin verse. His poem was loved and copied all over Christian Europe, and this copy was made in Wales in the ninth century. Its scholars filled its margins with notes, explaining hard Latin words in Welsh, which makes it one of the earliest witnesses to the Welsh language anywhere.",
  "## The englynion",
  "Twelve verses in Welsh were added to its pages, and they are the oldest Welsh poetry to survive in writing. Three of them are in the voice of a warrior who sits with his war band, but will not talk and will not laugh tonight, so heavy is his grief. They belong to the same world as the englynion of Llywarch Hen and Heledd: the old saga poetry of the Britons, spoken by heroes who have outlived their companions.",
  "The other nine are a hymn of praise to God the Creator, who made the wonders of the world. They show that by the ninth century the englyn was already a fine and practised art, the form the poets of Wales have used ever since.",
  "## Why it matters",
  "Most early Welsh poetry survives only in copies made centuries after it was composed. These verses were written down within the age of the poets themselves. They prove that the poetry of the Cynfeirdd, the first poets, is truly old, and they let us hear the Welsh of more than a thousand years ago.",
