  "|| Language | Latin",
  "## The first history written in Wales",
  "The History of the Britons, Historia Brittonum, was gathered in Gwynedd about 829, in the days of Merfyn Frych, the king who founded the line of Rhodri Mawr and Hywel Dda. It tells the story of the Island of Britain from Brutus to the Saxon wars: the coming of the Romans, the Britons’ long fight, Gwrtheyrn and the two dragons of Dinas Emrys, and the twelve battles of Arthur, the leader in battle of the kings of the Britons. It is the first book to name Arthur’s victories and the first to name the old poets Taliesin and Aneirin.[[cite:hb-giles]]",
  "Its author has long been known as Nennius, from a preface found in some copies, in which he says that he ‘made a heap of all that he found’. The writer’s own page on this site tells his story.",
  "## The manuscript in London",
  "The book survives in many copies, but one is above all precious: a manuscript written about 1100 and now in the British Library, numbered Harley 3859. With the History it keeps two other treasures of early Wales. One is the Annales Cambriae, the Annals of Wales, kept at St Davids, which run from 447 to 954 and record the battle of Badon and the strife of Camlan.[[cite:ac-wales]] The other is a set of family trees of the Welsh kings, drawn up in the days of Owain ap Hywel Dda, which trace his line back through Rhodri Mawr and Cunedda to the Britons of Roman times.",
