  "|| Kept at | National Library of Wales (NLW MS 17110E), bought in 1959",
  "## The story of a church",
  "The Book of Llandaf, Liber Landavensis, was made to tell the story of the church of Llandaf, near Cardiff, and to prove its right to its lands. Bishop Urban oversaw the work in the years after 1120, when he was in dispute with the bishops of St Davids and Hereford over the bounds of his diocese.[[cite:llandav-nlw]] To make his case, he gathered every old record his church possessed.",
  "So the book keeps the lives of the three saints of Llandaf, Dyfrig, Teilo and Euddogwy, and about a hundred and fifty charters, records of gifts of land to the church by the kings of Gwent, Glywysing and Ergyng over five hundred years.",
  "## The kings of Gwent",
  "The charters are a roll of honour of the kings of south-east Wales. Here are Tewdrig, who left his hermitage to win his last battle against the Saxons at Tintern, his son Meurig ap Tewdrig, and Meurig’s son Athrwys, who Welsh tradition holds was Arthur himself.[[cite:llandav-text]] Their names stand at the head of grants made with the witness of bishops and clergy, so that the book is one of the richest sources for the kings of early Wales.",
  "The charters mark the bounds of each gift in Welsh, by streams, hills, stones and old roads. Many of the names they give can still be found on the map of Monmouthshire and the Vale of Glamorgan today.",
  "## Its cover",
