  "## One scribe’s life’s work",
  "The Black Book of Carmarthen is the oldest surviving book written entirely in Welsh. Modern scholars believe it is the work of a single scribe, who wrote it at different times of his life, before and about the year 1250.[[cite:nlw-black]] Its pages are small, and its hand changes as the years pass, so that we can almost watch the scribe grow old over his work.",
  "## What it keeps",
  "It is above all a book of poetry. Here are the poems of Myrddin: Yr Afallennau, the Apple Trees, in which the prophet speaks to the sweet apple tree that hides him in the Caledonian forest, Yr Oianau, and the dialogue of Myrddin and Taliesin. Here is Pa Gur yv y Porthaur?, What man is the gatekeeper?, in which Arthur names his heroes to Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr, the porter of a fortress, Cai first among them. Here are the englynion for Geraint son of Erbin, who fell at Llongborth, and Englynion y Beddau, the Stanzas of the Graves, which tell where the heroes of the Island of Britain lie.",
  "Here too are the dialogue of Gwyddno Garanhir and Gwyn ap Nudd, lord of the Otherworld, the verses of the drowning of Cantre’r Gwaelod, poems of praise to the princes, among them the elegy for Madog ap Maredudd of Powys, and a small group of triads on the horses of the heroes.",
  "!! dormarch",
  "## From Carmarthen to Aberystwyth",
  "The book was found by Sir John Price of Brecon, the scholar who printed the first Welsh book, when he was searching the monasteries closed by Henry VIII. It was with the treasurer of St Davids, and it was said to have come from the priory at Carmarthen. William Salesbury, translator of the New Testament, wrote a note on one of its pages. In the seventeenth century it came to Robert Vaughan’s great library at Hengwrt, near Dolgellau, and from there it went to Peniarth, where it was numbered Peniarth 1, the first of all. Sir John Williams bought the collection in 1904 for the new National Library of Wales, where it is kept today.",
