  "|| Kept at | Jesus College, Oxford (MS 111), in the Bodleian Library",
  "## The greatest of the Welsh books",
  "The Red Book of Hergest is the largest and heaviest of all the medieval books in Welsh, a library in a single volume.[[cite:wiki-red-book]] It was written in the years after 1382 by Hywel Fychan ap Hywel Goch of Buellt and two other scribes, for Hopcyn ap Tomos ab Einion of Ynysforgan, the great patron of Welsh learning, whose house near Swansea was a meeting place for poets and scholars.",
  "## What it holds",
  "In its prose are the tales of the Mabinogion, Brut y Brenhinedd, the history of the kings of Britain, Brut y Tywysogion, the chronicle of the princes, and the Triads of the Island of Britain. In its poetry are the englynion of Llywarch Hen and of Heledd, the poems of Urien, the prophecies of Myrddin, and above all the great odes of the Poets of the Princes. And it keeps the remedies of Rhiwallon, the first of the Physicians of Myddfai, whose family were doctors for five hundred years.",
  "!! redbook",
  "## A book that travelled",
  "Hopcyn’s family kept it until his grandson lost his lands for joining a rising against Edward IV. By about 1465 it had passed to the Vaughans of Tretower, and the poet Lewys Glyn Cothi wrote two of his odes to Sir Thomas Vaughan and his sons into it there. Then it went to the Vaughans of Hergest Court, near Kington, where Sir John Price saw it in 1550, and from whom it takes its name. William Salesbury found it at Ludlow in the 1560s. By 1634 it belonged to the Mansels of Margam.",
  "It came at last to Thomas Wilkins, a clergyman and scholar of Glamorgan, and on 17 February 1701 his son gave it to Jesus College, Oxford, the college of the Welsh, where it has been ever since. The great scholar Edward Lhuyd borrowed it and kept it until his death.",
  "## A name that spread",
  "The book has given its name far beyond Wales. J. R. R. Tolkien, who loved the Welsh language, is thought by many to have borrowed it for the Red Book of Westmarch, the imagined book in which the stories of his hobbits were kept.",
